    "answer": "**Diagnosis:** h1\u2192h5 connectivity is failing because of a forwarding loop between **s1 and s2**, not because their direct physical link is down.\n\nEvidence:\n- A 5-packet ICMP test from h1 to h5 had **100% loss**.\n- Both ends of the direct path are healthy at the port level:\n  - s1 port 2 \u2194 s5 port 1 is enabled.\n  - s1 host port and s5 host port are enabled.\n- However, the installed destination-MAC forwarding rules for h5 are inconsistent:\n  - On **s1**, traffic destined for h5 (`00:...:05`) is sent to **port 1**, toward **s2**, rather than port 2 toward s5.\n  - On **s2**, that same destination is sent to **port 1**, back toward **s1**.\n  - Both rules recorded 7 packets, matching the failed h1\u2192h5 attempts and confirming that packets are circulating across s1\u2013s2.\n\nSo the fault is localised to the **baseline forwarding/flow programming for h5 on s1 and s2**. Correct forwarding should send h5-destined traffic:\n- **s1 \u2192 port 2** (to s5), or at minimum avoid the s1\u2013s2 loop.\n- If traffic is sent through s2, s2 must not return it to s1.\n\nSeparately, ONOS does not currently observe the configured **s3\u2013s4** link, but that is not required for h1\u2194h5 because the direct s1\u2013s5 link is active.",
